WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.200
How do you stop a lightsaber&nbsp;
mid-swing? Why, you use the Force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.460
of course. But who are the Star Wars characters&nbsp;
who have pulled off this incredible ability?

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:13.740
After returning to the Star Wars spotlight in&nbsp;
the last episode of Ahsoka, Ezra Bridger is back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.720
in action for the show's latest installment. In&nbsp;
"Dreams and Madness," Ezra teams up with Sabine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.980
Wren to take on the marauders of Peridea,&nbsp;
Grand Admiral Thrawn's night troopers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:28.500
and Shin Hati. Naturally, between her knowledge&nbsp;
of the Force and lightsaber abilities, Shin poses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.820
by far the biggest threat — but both Ezra&nbsp;
and Sabine are prepared for her attack.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.240
"She's like you, but lacks your sense of humor."

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:37.980
"Lightsaber?"

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:38.700
"Oh, yeah."

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.080
"Great."

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.760
Boldly, Ezra wields nothing but the Force&nbsp;
in his fight with Shin, even using it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.020
hold back one of her lightsaber swings. It's an&nbsp;
awesome moment in the midst of a frantic fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.400
and it proves just how strong Ezra has&nbsp;
become as a Force user. For reference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:59.700
in the Disney+ series Obi-Wan Kenobi, the fearsome&nbsp;
and immensely powerful Darth Vader uses a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.620
technique during his brief clash with Reva. Vader&nbsp;
uses the Force to prevent several of her strikes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.880
from landing and even prevents her specialized&nbsp;
double-bladed lightsaber from spinning.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.080
These aren't the only Force-users to have&nbsp;
nailed this impressive ability, however.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.660
Ezra and Vader's move has also been depicted on&nbsp;
the big screen via the Star Wars sequel trilogy.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.500
Though the return of fan favorites&nbsp;
such as Luke Skywalker, Han Solo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.540
and Princess Leia were a big selling point of the&nbsp;
sequel trilogy, these movies also boast a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.160
of franchise newcomers. Among these heroes&nbsp;
and villains are Rey Skywalker and Kylo Ren,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.900
who clash throughout The Force Awakens,&nbsp;
The Last Jedi, and The Rise of Skywalker.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.400
And it's their duel in the third film that&nbsp;
is particularly relevant here. In The Rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.840
of Skywalker, Rey and Kylo cross sabers in&nbsp;
the wreckage of the second Death Star. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.400
gigantic waves crash around them and their&nbsp;
situation becomes increasingly perilous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.480
they both manage to employ the same&nbsp;
technique as Ezra Bridger and Darth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.400
Vader. Early on in the fight, Rey briefly stops&nbsp;
Kylo Wren's red lightsaber by using the Force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.940
prompting him to do the same when&nbsp;
she attacks moments later. Seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:06.840
as Kylo is the grandson of Vader and Rey&nbsp;
is the granddaughter of Emperor Palpatine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.860
it's perhaps no surprise that they've both&nbsp;
mastered this immensely useful Force ability.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.660
And here's a little known fact, for you — at&nbsp;
one point, another famous Jedi was supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.220
use this power. Long before fans saw&nbsp;
Ezra Bridger, Darth Vader, Kylo Ren,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.620
or Rey Skywalker stop lightsaber swings with the&nbsp;
Force, Lucasfilm was hard at work wrapping up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.720
Skywalker saga. The Rise of Skywalker was the&nbsp;
film that ended it all, though the version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.520
Episode IX fans saw in theaters in 2019 was&nbsp;
vastly different from the one envisioned by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.260
the film's original director, Colin Trevorrow.&nbsp;
The script for Trevorrow's take on the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:45.420
known as Duel of the Fates, has been online for&nbsp;
some time. Some interesting features include Finn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.280
leading a full-blown stormtrooper uprising,&nbsp;
Kylo pursuing even more dark side power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.260
and a now-infamous stage direction that&nbsp;
claims Hux "lost the Star Wars." At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.880
this script also includes a pretty wild move&nbsp;
pulled off by the Force ghost of Luke Skywalker.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.500
As The Last Jedi ends with Luke passing into&nbsp;
the Force, it's no surprise that he's present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.340
in the script for Duel of the Fates. Luke&nbsp;
pops up here and there throughout the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:13.200
taunting Kylo from beyond the grave, and it&nbsp;
all culminates in him making his presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.000
truly felt during Kylo's final duel with Rey.&nbsp;
During this fight, Luke grabs hold of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.380
nephew's lightsaber by the flaming, crackling&nbsp;
blade — look deep enough on the internet and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:27.060
you'll even find the concept art drawn up for&nbsp;
this moment. It's an undeniably cool visual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.440
even if it is a move that living Star Wars&nbsp;
characters would be wise not to replicate.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.480
Clearly, this Force block ability is becoming more&nbsp;
and more commonplace in the franchise. We'll just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.920
have to wait and see if any more characters use&nbsp;
it as the Star Wars universe continues to expand.

